WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:00.923
<v Instructor>So let's talk</v>

2
00:00:00.923 --> 00:00:03.660
about AWS Resource Access Manager, or RAM.

3
00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:06.120
So you're going to share AWS resources that you own

4
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.890
with other AWS accounts.

5
00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:10.650
And with this you can do it from any account,

6
00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:13.140
or just accounts within your organization.

7
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.140
This is allowing you to avoid the resource duplication.

8
00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.910
So what can you share with RAM?

9
00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:20.130
You can share VPC Subnets, for example,

10
00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:22.710
which is very helpful and one of the best use case for RAM,

11
00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:24.510
which allows you to have all the resources

12
00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:26.400
launched within the same subnets.

13
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.410
And all these subnets

14
00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:30.990
then must be from the same AWS organizations.

15
00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:33.180
You cannot share the default security groups

16
00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.220
and the default VPC.

17
00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.320
The participants can manage their own resources

18
00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.050
within the subnets, but the participants cannot view,

19
00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.700
modify, or delete resources

20
00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:44.190
that belong to other participants or the owner.

21
00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:46.320
So they only share the networking, really.

22
00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.630
You can also share a transit gateway

23
00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:52.920
or Route 53 Resolver Rules and a DNS Firewall Rule Groups,

24
00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.240
and so on.

25
00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.460
Or license manager configurations.

26
00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.320
There's other stuff you can share. I'm just gonna do a list.

27
00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:01.650
So Aura DB Clusters, ACM Private Authority,

28
00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.960
Certificate Authority, CodeBuild Projects,

29
00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.660
EC2 Dedicated Hosts, Capacity Reservations,

30
00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.060
Glue Catalog, Database, and Table,

31
00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.160
Network Firewall Policies, Resource Groups,

32
00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.317
System Manager, System Incidents, Outpost,

33
00:01:13.317 --> 00:01:15.030
and the list goes on and on and on,

34
00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:16.620
and it will not update it, okay?

35
00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:18.360
But one of the most important use case

36
00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.150
from an exam perspective for Resource Access Manager

37
00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:22.590
is for the VPC.

38
00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:26.970
So in this example, we have a VPC and there's a VPC owner

39
00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:28.080
and it has a private subnet,

40
00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:29.790
but this VPC is going to be shared

41
00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:31.560
with account one and account two.

42
00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.480
So each account is going to be responsible

43
00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.280
for its own resources, okay?

44
00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:36.630
They cannot view and modify

45
00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:38.940
or delete other resources in other accounts.

46
00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:41.490
So for example, if I deploy an EC2 instance

47
00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:42.900
and ALB in account one,

48
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.480
then an account two will have an EC2 instance.

49
00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.250
They can communicate with one another,

50
00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:48.780
but they cannot view each other, okay,

51
00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.490
because they are in separate accounts.

52
00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:51.660
And then your database

53
00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:54.960
may sit in a private subnet of the VPC owner.

54
00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:55.860
And so network is shared.

55
00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:58.440
That means that all these things can talk to each other

56
00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.840
and these applications can access each other

57
00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:01.980
using private IPs.

58
00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:06.000
So this avoid the needs, for example, to do a VPC peering.

59
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.210
Now security group can also be referenced across accounts

60
00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:11.910
for maximum security, which is very helpful.

61
00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:13.680
Now, the use cases for this

62
00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:17.070
is that applications within the same trust boundaries

63
00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:18.750
can be deployed within the same VPC

64
00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.030
and they will simplify your network.

65
00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:21.990
And if you have applications

66
00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:25.800
with a high degree of interconnectivity, yet, again,

67
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.820
you will have a much simplified network set up

68
00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.080
because you only have one VPC.

69
00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:33.720
And so by default, any application deployed within it

70
00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.060
can talk with each other.

71
00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:38.460
Another example of what can be shared

72
00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:42.000
by Resource Access Manager is a managed prefix list.

73
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:43.590
So what is a managed prefix list?

74
00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:47.970
Well, it is a set of one or more CIDR blocks,

75
00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:49.620
and it makes it easier to maintain

76
00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:52.200
and configure security group rules and route tables.

77
00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:53.670
So here's an example.

78
00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:55.650
Account A has a prefix list,

79
00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:59.610
and in it you have the CIDR 1, CIDR 2, CIDR 3.

80
00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:01.080
And these are set of rules, for example,

81
00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:04.890
that represent your internal network of your company.

82
00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:07.230
And say you want to apply these rules,

83
00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:10.080
these prefixes, to many different security groups.

84
00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:13.140
So instead of having them all in all security groups,

85
00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:15.360
what you do is that you create a prefix list

86
00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.040
and then that prefix list can be referenced

87
00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:18.540
by a security group.

88
00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:20.490
So on top of it, this can be shared.

89
00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.820
So if they're shared using RAM to another account,

90
00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:26.070
then a security group inbound rules

91
00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:28.080
can look as simple as this.

92
00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.290
Where you say, well, SSH is allowed on port 22

93
00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:34.080
from the source Prefix List A.

94
00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:36.600
And so that if you are using, for example,

95
00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:39.300
your own customer managed prefix list,

96
00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.490
then you define the CIDRs

97
00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:44.070
and then you can share it with other accounts' organizations

98
00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:45.660
and then you can modify them.

99
00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:47.880
And if you modify your prefix list,

100
00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:50.520
automatically all the security groups

101
00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:52.560
that were leveraging that prefix list,

102
00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:53.940
or all the route tables

103
00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:55.590
that were leveraging that prefix list,

104
00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:57.720
are going to be updated at the same time,

105
00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:01.290
which gives you a central management of your prefixes,

106
00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:02.520
and so on.

107
00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:05.280
You also have a second kind of prefix list,

108
00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:08.430
which is the AWS-Managed Prefix List.

109
00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:11.580
So this time the CIDRs are defined by AWS

110
00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:14.940
for their own services, and you cannot create, modify,

111
00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:16.410
share, or delete them,

112
00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:20.250
but I wanted you to know about them in this slide anyway.

113
00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:23.130
Something else you can share with Resource Access Manager

114
00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:25.980
is the Route 53 Outbound Resolver.

115
00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:26.813
So this allows you

116
00:04:26.813 --> 00:04:28.931
to scale your following rules to your DNS

117
00:04:28.931 --> 00:04:30.441
in case you have a hybrid setup,

118
00:04:30.441 --> 00:04:33.117
and you have multiple accounts and multiple VPC.

119
00:04:33.117 --> 00:04:34.739
So the idea is that in your main account,

120
00:04:34.739 --> 00:04:38.130
you will define the resolver rule for Route 53.

121
00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:40.350
So you have your forwarding rules

122
00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:44.250
that have the domain names that you want and the target IPs,

123
00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.830
and then you would share those with other accounts

124
00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:49.860
so that after you share it, the other account accepts it,

125
00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:52.560
then they can be associated with other VPC,

126
00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:54.630
and any other VPC that have these rules

127
00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:57.570
can then resolve the domain names you've specified.

128
00:04:57.570 --> 00:04:58.403
So this allows you

129
00:04:58.403 --> 00:05:03.060
to have a centrally managed resolver rules for your DNS.

130
00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:05.760
Okay, so that's it for Resource Access Manager.

131
00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:08.760
I hope you liked it, and I will see you in the next lecture.

